configure: optimise newlib outside debug builds

The debug branch of CFLAGS_FOR_TARGET says -O0 explicitly; the other one
says nothing, and nothing means GCC's default, which is also -O0. So every
C function in newlib — libm entirely, and libc apart from the routines
written in assembler — is built unoptimised in a release build, and the
generated code shows it: sinl() stores its argument on the stack and reads
it straight back before doing anything.

That matters to anything calling the maths library in a loop. Basilisk II's
68881 emulation calls sinl, cosl, tanl, atanl, expl, logl, powl and sqrtl
for the FPU instructions of the same names, so an emulated floating-point
benchmark was measuring an unoptimised libm.

-O2, as the debug branch implies was intended, and -ffp-contract=off so
that the double-precision functions keep exactly the results they had:
fdlibm-derived code is written for IEEE operations one at a time, and at
-O2 GCC would otherwise fuse multiplies and adds.

diff --git a/configure b/configure
index b87aeb1..e490dc4 100755
--- a/configure
+++ b/configure
@@ -357,7 +357,7 @@ if [ $DEBUG -eq 1 ]
 then
     CFLAGS_FOR_TARGET="$ARCH -D__circle__ -DRASPPI=$RASPBERRYPI $CIRCLE_DEFINES -O0 -g -Wno-parentheses $KASAN_CFLAGS"
 else
-    CFLAGS_FOR_TARGET="$ARCH -D__circle__ -DRASPPI=$RASPBERRYPI $CIRCLE_DEFINES -g -Wno-parentheses $KASAN_CFLAGS"
+    CFLAGS_FOR_TARGET="$ARCH -D__circle__ -DRASPPI=$RASPBERRYPI $CIRCLE_DEFINES -O2 -ffp-contract=off -g -Wno-parentheses $KASAN_CFLAGS"
 fi
 if [ $CLANG -eq 1 ]
 then
